WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.893
Model-serving performance depends on several interacting factors: model size, quantization,

00:00:03.893 --> 00:00:08.825
context length, available memory, CPU, GPU, disk speed, thermal behavior, and concurrent use.

00:00:08.825 --> 00:00:13.377
A model that feels responsive for one user on a workstation may

00:00:13.377 --> 00:00:16.032
not be suitable for a shared server,

00:00:16.032 --> 00:00:19.067
a heavier prompt workflow, or multiple simultaneous clients.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:23.999
Operators should watch memory, disk, CPU, GPU, and temperature while evaluating a model.

00:00:23.999 --> 00:00:28.171
Memory pressure can cause slow responses, failed loads, or system instability.

00:00:28.171 --> 00:00:31.206
Disk pressure can affect model pulls and logging.

00:00:31.206 --> 00:00:34.241
CPU and GPU pressure can change latency expectations.

00:00:34.241 --> 00:00:40.310
Thermal throttling can make performance worse over time even if the first few requests look fine.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:41.828
Context length also matters.

00:00:41.828 --> 00:00:46.380
Longer prompts and larger conversation histories require more work and more memory.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.932
Keep-alive behavior and loaded models can improve responsiveness because a model does

00:00:50.932 --> 00:00:53.587
not need to load for every request,

00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:56.243
but they can also hold scarce memory.

00:00:56.243 --> 00:01:01.174
The best setting depends on whether the environment is optimized for interactive use,

00:01:01.174 --> 00:01:03.071
occasional requests, or shared workloads.

00:01:03.071 --> 00:01:07.623
Capacity planning should be based on observed workloads rather than wishful assumptions.

00:01:07.623 --> 00:01:11.037
Measure representative prompts, expected concurrency, response latency, resource use,

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.175
and failure behavior.

00:01:12.175 --> 00:01:14.831
Then document what the environment can support.

00:01:14.831 --> 00:01:20.900
Performance planning turns local AI from a pleasant demo into an operational service with realistic expectations.
